<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">temperature</ansi>

The air around you has a temperature, and each member of your company
has an exposure meter that tracks whether it's too cold or too hot for
them. Exposure runs 0 (comfortable) to 100 in either direction: negative
for cold, positive for heat.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Staying comfortable</ansi>

What you wear gives warmth, which shifts your comfortable range. With
nothing on, you're comfortable outdoors only in a narrow, mild band;
a full set of warm clothing pushes the cold edge of that range well
below freezing while still keeping the hot edge close by. Being indoors
in a lit, furnished room keeps you warm regardless of what you're
wearing. A lit fire warms the air around it by 10 degrees: welcome, but
on snow at night you still need your clothes. Caves and dungeons hold a
steady temperature of their own.

When the air is outside your comfortable range, your exposure meter
climbs toward a ceiling set by how far outside it you are. Getting back
inside that range, especially somewhere sheltered or by a fire, brings
it back down.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">The cost</ansi>

The deeper your exposure, the more it costs you:

  - Cold exposure of any real degree drains your Fatigue every so
    often, on top of anything else spending it.
  - Heat exposure drains your Thirst.
  - Severe exposure, in either direction, starts doing you real
    damage; critical exposure does much more, and can kill.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">The </ansi><ansi fg="command">temperature</ansi><ansi fg="yellow-bold"> command</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">temperature</ansi>
  Shows the air temperature here and why (weather, night, a fire),
  your clothing's warmth and the range it keeps you comfortable in,
  and how exposed you and each companion currently are: comfortable, a
  little cold or warm, or somewhere on the chilled-to-freezing or
  overheated-to-heatstroke scale.

A warming draught or a cooling salve slows one member's exposure for 15
minutes (<ansi fg="command">help camp supplies</ansi>).

See also: <ansi fg="command">help weather</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help survival</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help strain</ansi>,
<ansi fg="command">help camp</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help conditions</ansi>.

Frostbitten or worse adds one round to new chants and sling shots, in addition to existing cold stat penalties. See help battlefield.
